{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FdaDoUwPaaGrWcwk1KQjfd","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the paper, its experimental evidence, framing, related work positioning, and development history. Let me compose my review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: R2 (Novelty + Related Work)\n\n**Paper:** \"Where Do Actions Come From? A Representational Decomposition of Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\n---\n\n### 1. Novelty and Significance\n\n**Core contribution: representational decomposition of VLAs.** The idea of comparing frozen vision encoder outputs against LLM hidden states using RSA and probes is straightforward but has not been done before in this specific configuration. The novelty is moderate rather than high -- the tools (RSA, CKA, linear probes) are entirely standard, and the experimental design (trained vs. untrained control) is a well-known paradigm from neuroscience-inspired interpretability. What is new is applying this combination to a VLA pipeline with careful component isolation.\n\n**\"Geometric-functional dissociation.\"** This is the most genuinely novel insight: DINO achieves R-squared values comparable to SigLIP+Projector (0.712 vs 0.706, p=0.22) yet has vastly lower RSA (0.388 vs 0.615). This separation between \"information presence\" and \"geometric alignment with action structure\" is conceptually interesting and, to my knowledge, has not been explicitly demonstrated in this domain. However, the insight is somewhat obvious upon reflection -- self-supervised vision encoders encode rich spatial information from which actions can be decoded, while vision-language pretraining happens to organize that information in a way that correlates with action structure. The finding is empirically clean but conceptually modest.\n\n**\"Routing vs. creating\" framing.** This is largely a repackaging of the observation that distributed representations are training-invariant (p=0.20, n.s.) while readout concentration is training-dependent (delta R-squared=0.045, p<0.0001). The framing is evocative but the underlying observation -- that fine-tuning teaches attention patterns to aggregate pre-existing information rather than injecting new information into intermediate representations -- is consistent with well-established understanding of how fine-tuning operates in large language models (e.g., the \"lazy training\" regime literature, work on representation similarity across fine-tuning in NLP). The paper does not adequately connect to this broader literature on what fine-tuning does to representations.\n\n**Would findings change practitioner behavior?** The practical implication (\"invest in vision encoder pretraining, not larger LLMs\") is somewhat actionable but also largely confirmatory of what the VLA community was already converging toward (Grant et al. 2026 behavioral evidence, the shift toward smaller action experts in pi0.5/SmolVLA architectures). The readout concentration finding has the most potential practical value for action head design, but this is not developed.\n\n**Overall novelty assessment:** The paper presents a well-executed empirical analysis with one genuinely interesting finding (geometric-functional dissociation) and one confirmatory-but-useful finding (training teaches routing). The contribution is primarily empirical characterization rather than mechanistic discovery or methodological advance.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Related Work Coverage\n\n**Strengths:**\n- The paper cites the most relevant concurrent work (Grant et al. 2026, Hou 2026 LangGap, Swann et al. 2026 SAE, Haon et al. CoRL 2025, Rizzardo et al. 2026).\n- Positioning relative to Grant et al. 2026 is accurate: behavioral vs. representational evidence, complementary not competing. The OFT vs. standard fine-tuning difference is appropriately acknowledged.\n- Methodological references (Kriegeskorte 2008, Kornblith 2019) are appropriate.\n\n**Gaps and Missing References:**\n\n(a) **Mechanistic interpretability of LLMs generally.** The paper should cite the probing literature in NLP more comprehensively:\n- Belinkov & Glass (2019) \"Analysis Methods in Neural Language Processing: A Survey\" -- canonical reference for probing methodology.\n- Hewitt & Manning (2019) \"A Structural Probe for Finding Syntax in Word Representations\" -- relevant to the probe complexity control issue the authors themselves encountered (D018).\n- Voita et al. (2019) on attention head pruning and information flow -- directly relevant to the \"routing\" claim.\n- The \"lazy training\" / \"representation preservation under fine-tuning\" literature: Aghajanyan et al. (2021) \"Intrinsic Dimensionality Explains the Effectiveness of Language Model Fine-Tuning\" -- the finding that fine-tuning operates in a low-dimensional subspace is a precursor to \"routing not creating.\"\n\n(b) **RSA/CKA applied to other vision-language models.** The paper uses RSA/CKA as tools but does not contextualize how these methods have been applied to VLMs:\n- Raghu et al. (2021) \"Do Vision Transformers See Like Convolutional Neural Networks?\" -- CKA on vision transformers.\n- Huh et al. (2024) \"Platonic Representation Hypothesis\" -- representation convergence across modalities, directly relevant to why DINO and SigLIP have similar R-squared.\n- No mention of representational alignment studies in multimodal models (e.g., Liang et al. studies on modality gap in CLIP-like models).\n\n(c) **Other VLA architectures beyond OpenVLA.** The paper acknowledges testing only OpenVLA, but should more explicitly discuss:\n- pi0/pi0.5 (flow matching, not autoregressive) -- structurally different action generation.\n- RT-2-X, Octo -- mentioned but not discussed in terms of whether findings would transfer.\n- The new dual-pathway architectures (pi0.5, SmolVLA, GR00T) where Grant et al. found different dynamics.\n\n(d) **Probing studies in vision.** The geometric-functional dissociation finding should be connected to:\n- Li et al. (2016) \"Convergent Learning\" -- different networks learn similar features.\n- The broader representation alignment literature showing that different training objectives produce representations with similar decodability but different geometric structure.\n\n**Positioning accuracy:**\n- The claim that \"no work has performed representational decomposition\" is accurate for VLAs specifically but undersells the broader probing/RSA literature that has done analogous decompositions in LLMs and VLMs.\n- The differentiation from Lu et al. (2025) is correctly stated (component-level vs. layer-level) but could be sharpened -- Lu et al. only probe trained models, while this paper's key contribution is the trained vs. untrained contrast.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Framing and Claims\n\n**Claim 1: \"VL-pretrained encoder dominance\"** -- The word \"dominance\" is overclaimed given the evidence. RSA shows SigLIP+Projector has higher geometric alignment with action space than the full VLA. But: (a) only one VLA architecture is tested; (b) RSA is one metric of \"dominance\" -- CKA actually shows the LLM having higher values than the projector; (c) the functional probe shows the readout token SURPASSES the projector (0.731 vs 0.706). So \"dominance\" depends entirely on which metric and which aggregation. The paper should use more precise language: \"geometric source\" rather than \"dominant.\"\n\n**Claim 2: \"LLM redistributes, training-dependently\"** -- This is well-calibrated to the evidence. The trained vs. untrained readout difference (delta=0.045, p<0.0001) is statistically robust. The \"redistribution\" framing correctly captures that image tokens lose and the readout token gains.\n\n**Claim 3: \"Training routes, not creates\"** -- This claim is the most problematic:\n- The non-significance of the RSA difference (p=0.20) does not prove equivalence. A non-significant difference with n=10 tasks is weak evidence of invariance. A proper equivalence test (TOST) is not reported.\n- The evidence for \"routing\" (readout concentration) is a 4.5 percentage point improvement in R-squared. This is statistically significant but practically small -- it accounts for an incremental improvement over what an untrained model already partially achieves (0.686).\n- The title-level claim \"training routes, not creates\" is bolder than the evidence warrants. A more defensible version would be: \"training primarily improves information routing with negligible change to distributed representations.\"\n\n**Limitations discussion:** The paper acknowledges single-architecture limitation (appropriate). However, it does not adequately discuss:\n- The untrained control initializes a RANDOM LLM while preserving the frozen encoder. This is an extreme comparison -- the \"untrained\" model has never seen any text or images through its LLM. The claim would be much stronger if comparing early-stage vs. late-stage fine-tuning, or comparing few-shot vs. full fine-tuning.\n- RSA on 10 tasks with 500 frames each -- the statistical power for detecting small effects may be limited.\n- LIBERO is a simulation benchmark with limited visual and motor complexity.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Impact and Broader Significance\n\n**Would this paper influence future VLA research/design?** Partially. The finding that vision encoder pretraining quality matters most is confirmatory of existing trends. The practical implication for action head design (concentrate information into readout tokens) is actionable but incremental. The geometric-functional dissociation finding could influence encoder selection criteria (optimize for geometric alignment, not just decodability), but this needs more development.\n\n**Practical implications:**\n- \"Choose VL-pretrained encoders over vision-only\" -- already standard practice in most VLA designs.\n- \"LLM routing architecture matters\" -- underspecified; what specifically should change?\n- \"Invest in encoder pretraining not LLM fine-tuning\" -- potentially misleading since the paper does not test what happens with MORE fine-tuning data, only the binary trained/untrained contrast.\n\nThe impact is primarily to the interpretability/analysis community rather than practitioners.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Weaknesses and Questions\n\n**Major Weaknesses:**\n\nW1. **Single architecture, single benchmark suite.** OpenVLA is one architecture with one specific design (frozen SigLIP, Llama-2 7B, autoregressive action tokens). Modern VLAs like pi0.5 use flow matching with separate expert pathways. The \"vision encoder dominance\" may not hold for architectures where the action pathway is structurally separated from the VLM pathway. Testing even one additional architecture (even Octo, which is open-source) would substantially strengthen the claims.\n\nW2. **Extreme untrained control.** Comparing a fully fine-tuned model against a randomly-initialized LLM is the weakest possible test of \"does training change representations.\" A more informative comparison would be: base pretrained Llama-2 (language-only pretraining) vs. fine-tuned OpenVLA. The current comparison conflates \"VLA fine-tuning effect\" with \"language pretraining + VLA fine-tuning effect\" in the trained condition, vs. \"nothing\" in the untrained condition. The fact that representations are similar despite this extreme manipulation is LESS surprising than it would be if comparing pretrained vs. fine-tuned -- because the frozen encoder+projector dominate input, both models receive identical inputs and are thus constrained to produce similar early-layer representations.\n\nW3. **Confirmatory of concurrent work.** Grant et al. (2026) already established \"visual pathway dominates\" and \"language encoded but not used\" across 6 architectures with real-world deployment. This paper confirms the same finding at a different analysis level but does not substantially advance the understanding beyond Grant's conclusions. The geometric-functional dissociation is the main differentiator but occupies relatively little of the paper.\n\nW4. **RSA metric limitations.** The RSA metric measures Spearman correlation between distance structures. It is sensitive to the specific distance metric used and the sampling of conditions. The paper reports high variance across tasks (CLIP ranges 0.235-0.783). The reliance on a single geometric metric as the primary evidence, especially when CKA REVERSES the ordering, is methodologically fragile.\n\nW5. **Language instruction finding is trivially expected.** The paper reports language instructions have no effect on representations. But the trained model was fine-tuned on LIBERO-10 where (as Hou 2026 and others documented) each scene uniquely determines the task, making language instructions unnecessary. The model learned to ignore instructions because they were redundant in training. This is not evidence that VLAs generally cannot use language -- it is evidence that this specific model on this specific benchmark does not.\n\n**Minor Weaknesses:**\n\nW6. The p=0.08 for CLIP vs SigLIP+Projector comparison is presented as \"statistically indistinguishable.\" With n=10 tasks, this is simply underpowered. The CI (0.442-0.660 for CLIP) is extremely wide.\n\nW7. The paper does not report what happens at the action head itself -- only the readout token before the action head. The final transformation from readout to discrete action tokens may further modify the picture.\n\nW8. \"Cross-benchmark ratios 0.95-0.97\" -- this sounds like a replication, but LIBERO-Spatial and LIBERO-Goal share the same codebase, similar visual style, and related task structures. True generalization would require a different simulator or real-world data.\n\n**Questions for Authors:**\n\nQ1. What does the RSA look like for a base Llama-2 pretrained model (not fine-tuned for actions, but not randomly initialized either)? This would isolate the contribution of VLA fine-tuning from the contribution of language pretraining.\n\nQ2. The untrained readout R-squared is 0.686 at L32 -- which layers achieve the best untrained image_mean R-squared? The paper reports 0.714 at L0. Why does the very first layer of a random LLM achieve HIGHER R-squared than the projector (0.714 > 0.706)? This seems to contradict the \"degradation\" narrative.\n\nQ3. If we use the action probabilities from the actual action head (not a learned probe), what is the action prediction accuracy of trained vs. untrained models? This grounds the representation-level analysis in actual behavioral differences.\n\nQ4. The CKA ordering reversal (untrained > trained > projector) is described but not adequately explained. Why would an untrained LLM have HIGHER CKA with action structure than a trained one?\n\nQ5. For the CLIP control: CLIP uses a different tokenization (14x14 patch vs SigLIP's 14x14) and different dimensionality. Was the comparison controlled for representation dimensionality?\n\n---\n\n### 6. Score and Confidence\n\n**Overall Score: 4 (Weak Accept)**\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High confidence)**\n\n**Justification:** The paper presents a systematic and methodologically sound empirical analysis with one genuinely novel finding (geometric-functional dissociation) and several confirmatory findings that add representational-level evidence to an emerging picture. The experimental design is careful with appropriate controls (null controls, bootstrap CIs, multiple metrics). However, the novelty is diminished by (a) heavy confirmation of concurrent work (Grant et al. 2026), (b) an extreme untrained control that makes the \"invariance\" finding less surprising, (c) single-architecture scope, and (d) the core \"routing not creating\" framing being a restatement of known fine-tuning dynamics in LLMs. The related work coverage is good for VLA-specific literature but misses important connections to the broader probing/fine-tuning literature. The paper is above the reject threshold due to the geometric-functional dissociation insight and careful experimental methodology, but below a clear accept due to limited novelty and generalizability concerns. The paper would benefit from (1) testing at least one additional architecture, (2) including a base-pretrained LLM control, and (3) more explicitly positioning the \"routing\" finding within the broader fine-tuning literature.\n\n---\n\n### Critical Files for Implementation\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/literature_map.yaml\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/paper_framing.md\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/decisions.yaml\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/writing_plan.yaml\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/memory.md"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f97ff991-0e8f-42e6-bed2-f413306c2d1e","uuid":"9e6e2b78-de46-440a-a894-744390beb8f5","timestamp":"2026-05-16T01:44:09.895Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**. Evaluate the paper below using a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Where Do Actions Come From? A Representational Decomposition of Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\nThe paper performs a systematic representational analysis of OpenVLA, decomposing contributions from the frozen vision encoder (SigLIP), projector, and LLM backbone. Using RSA, CKA, and functional action probes across two LIBERO benchmarks and three vision encoders (SigLIP, CLIP ViT-L, DINOv2), they establish three findings:\n\n1. **VL-pretrained encoder dominance**: SigLIP+Projector achieves highest RSA (ρ=0.615), CLIP ViT-L is statistically indistinguishable (ρ=0.553, p=0.08), DINO significantly lower (ρ=0.388). But all encoders achieve comparable action probe R²≈0.70 — geometric-functional dissociation.\n\n2. **LLM redistributes, training-dependently**: RSA drops from projector (0.615) to LLM image_mean (0.501), but trained readout token achieves R²=0.731 > projector 0.706. Untrained readout R²=0.686 < projector — concentration is learned.\n\n3. **Training routes, not creates**: Trained vs untrained RSA difference is non-significant (0.501 vs 0.485, p=0.20). But readout concentration gap is large (ΔR²=0.045, p<0.0001). Language instructions have no effect.\n\nAll findings replicate on LIBERO-Spatial with cross-benchmark ratios 0.95–0.97.\n\n**Related work covered:**\n- VLA models: OpenVLA, RT-2, Prismatic VLMs\n- Behavioral analysis: Grant et al. (2026) visual pathway dominance; Hou (2026, LangGap); Fei et al. (2025, LIBERO-Plus) — language instruction insensitivity\n- Mechanistic interpretability: Haon et al. (2025) activation steering; Lu et al. (2025) linear probes; Swann et al. (2026) sparse autoencoders; Molinari et al. (2025) world representations; Rizzardo et al. (2026) feature observability\n- VLM fine-tuning: Fang (2025) fine-tuning erodes reasoning\n- Methods: Kornblith et al. (2019) CKA; Radford et al. (2021) CLIP; Oquab et al. (2024) DINOv2\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions and provide detailed comments:\n\n### 1. Novelty and Significance\n- How novel is the core contribution (representational decomposition of VLAs)?\n- Is the \"geometric-functional dissociation\" a genuinely new insight?\n- Is \"routing vs creating\" framing novel or a restatement of known results?\n- Does the paper advance understanding of VLAs meaningfully?\n- Would the findings change how practitioners design VLAs?\n\n### 2. Related Work Coverage\n- Are key related works adequately cited and compared?\n- Missing references? Consider: (a) mechanistic interpretability of LLMs generally, (b) RSA/CKA applied to other vision-language models, (c) other VLA architectures beyond OpenVLA, (d) probing studies in NLP/vision\n- Is the positioning relative to Grant et al. (2026), Hou (2026), etc. accurate?\n- Does the paper clearly articulate what it adds beyond existing behavioral/mechanistic analyses?\n\n### 3. Framing and Claims\n- Are the claims well-calibrated to the evidence?\n- Is \"VL-pretrained encoder dominance\" overclaimed given only OpenVLA is tested?\n- Is \"training routes not creates\" a strong enough claim given the evidence?\n- Are limitations adequately discussed?\n\n### 4. Impact and Broader Significance\n- Would this paper influence future VLA research/design?\n- Are the practical implications (vision encoder selection, routing architecture) well-supported?\n\n### 5. Weaknesses and Questions\n- List specific weaknesses\n- Raise specific questions for the authors\n\n### 6. Score and Confidence\n- Overall score (1-6)\n- Confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n- Brief justification\n\nOutput a complete structured review. Do NOT write any code or make edits. This is pure analysis.\n","agentId":"a145061b2259afe10","agentType":"Plan","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the paper, its experimental evidence, framing, related work positioning, and development history. Let me compose my review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: R2 (Novelty + Related Work)\n\n**Paper:** \"Where Do Actions Come From? A Representational Decomposition of Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\n---\n\n### 1. Novelty and Significance\n\n**Core contribution: representational decomposition of VLAs.** The idea of comparing frozen vision encoder outputs against LLM hidden states using RSA and probes is straightforward but has not been done before in this specific configuration. The novelty is moderate rather than high -- the tools (RSA, CKA, linear probes) are entirely standard, and the experimental design (trained vs. untrained control) is a well-known paradigm from neuroscience-inspired interpretability. What is new is applying this combination to a VLA pipeline with careful component isolation.\n\n**\"Geometric-functional dissociation.\"** This is the most genuinely novel insight: DINO achieves R-squared values comparable to SigLIP+Projector (0.712 vs 0.706, p=0.22) yet has vastly lower RSA (0.388 vs 0.615). This separation between \"information presence\" and \"geometric alignment with action structure\" is conceptually interesting and, to my knowledge, has not been explicitly demonstrated in this domain. However, the insight is somewhat obvious upon reflection -- self-supervised vision encoders encode rich spatial information from which actions can be decoded, while vision-language pretraining happens to organize that information in a way that correlates with action structure. The finding is empirically clean but conceptually modest.\n\n**\"Routing vs. creating\" framing.** This is largely a repackaging of the observation that distributed representations are training-invariant (p=0.20, n.s.) while readout concentration is training-dependent (delta R-squared=0.045, p<0.0001). The framing is evocative but the underlying observation -- that fine-tuning teaches attention patterns to aggregate pre-existing information rather than injecting new information into intermediate representations -- is consistent with well-established understanding of how fine-tuning operates in large language models (e.g., the \"lazy training\" regime literature, work on representation similarity across fine-tuning in NLP). The paper does not adequately connect to this broader literature on what fine-tuning does to representations.\n\n**Would findings change practitioner behavior?** The practical implication (\"invest in vision encoder pretraining, not larger LLMs\") is somewhat actionable but also largely confirmatory of what the VLA community was already converging toward (Grant et al. 2026 behavioral evidence, the shift toward smaller action experts in pi0.5/SmolVLA architectures). The readout concentration finding has the most potential practical value for action head design, but this is not developed.\n\n**Overall novelty assessment:** The paper presents a well-executed empirical analysis with one genuinely interesting finding (geometric-functional dissociation) and one confirmatory-but-useful finding (training teaches routing). The contribution is primarily empirical characterization rather than mechanistic discovery or methodological advance.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Related Work Coverage\n\n**Strengths:**\n- The paper cites the most relevant concurrent work (Grant et al. 2026, Hou 2026 LangGap, Swann et al. 2026 SAE, Haon et al. CoRL 2025, Rizzardo et al. 2026).\n- Positioning relative to Grant et al. 2026 is accurate: behavioral vs. representational evidence, complementary not competing. The OFT vs. standard fine-tuning difference is appropriately acknowledged.\n- Methodological references (Kriegeskorte 2008, Kornblith 2019) are appropriate.\n\n**Gaps and Missing References:**\n\n(a) **Mechanistic interpretability of LLMs generally.** The paper should cite the probing literature in NLP more comprehensively:\n- Belinkov & Glass (2019) \"Analysis Methods in Neural Language Processing: A Survey\" -- canonical reference for probing methodology.\n- Hewitt & Manning (2019) \"A Structural Probe for Finding Syntax in Word Representations\" -- relevant to the probe complexity control issue the authors themselves encountered (D018).\n- Voita et al. (2019) on attention head pruning and information flow -- directly relevant to the \"routing\" claim.\n- The \"lazy training\" / \"representation preservation under fine-tuning\" literature: Aghajanyan et al. (2021) \"Intrinsic Dimensionality Explains the Effectiveness of Language Model Fine-Tuning\" -- the finding that fine-tuning operates in a low-dimensional subspace is a precursor to \"routing not creating.\"\n\n(b) **RSA/CKA applied to other vision-language models.** The paper uses RSA/CKA as tools but does not contextualize how these methods have been applied to VLMs:\n- Raghu et al. (2021) \"Do Vision Transformers See Like Convolutional Neural Networks?\" -- CKA on vision transformers.\n- Huh et al. (2024) \"Platonic Representation Hypothesis\" -- representation convergence across modalities, directly relevant to why DINO and SigLIP have similar R-squared.\n- No mention of representational alignment studies in multimodal models (e.g., Liang et al. studies on modality gap in CLIP-like models).\n\n(c) **Other VLA architectures beyond OpenVLA.** The paper acknowledges testing only OpenVLA, but should more explicitly discuss:\n- pi0/pi0.5 (flow matching, not autoregressive) -- structurally different action generation.\n- RT-2-X, Octo -- mentioned but not discussed in terms of whether findings would transfer.\n- The new dual-pathway architectures (pi0.5, SmolVLA, GR00T) where Grant et al. found different dynamics.\n\n(d) **Probing studies in vision.** The geometric-functional dissociation finding should be connected to:\n- Li et al. (2016) \"Convergent Learning\" -- different networks learn similar features.\n- The broader representation alignment literature showing that different training objectives produce representations with similar decodability but different geometric structure.\n\n**Positioning accuracy:**\n- The claim that \"no work has performed representational decomposition\" is accurate for VLAs specifically but undersells the broader probing/RSA literature that has done analogous decompositions in LLMs and VLMs.\n- The differentiation from Lu et al. (2025) is correctly stated (component-level vs. layer-level) but could be sharpened -- Lu et al. only probe trained models, while this paper's key contribution is the trained vs. untrained contrast.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Framing and Claims\n\n**Claim 1: \"VL-pretrained encoder dominance\"** -- The word \"dominance\" is overclaimed given the evidence. RSA shows SigLIP+Projector has higher geometric alignment with action space than the full VLA. But: (a) only one VLA architecture is tested; (b) RSA is one metric of \"dominance\" -- CKA actually shows the LLM having higher values than the projector; (c) the functional probe shows the readout token SURPASSES the projector (0.731 vs 0.706). So \"dominance\" depends entirely on which metric and which aggregation. The paper should use more precise language: \"geometric source\" rather than \"dominant.\"\n\n**Claim 2: \"LLM redistributes, training-dependently\"** -- This is well-calibrated to the evidence. The trained vs. untrained readout difference (delta=0.045, p<0.0001) is statistically robust. The \"redistribution\" framing correctly captures that image tokens lose and the readout token gains.\n\n**Claim 3: \"Training routes, not creates\"** -- This claim is the most problematic:\n- The non-significance of the RSA difference (p=0.20) does not prove equivalence. A non-significant difference with n=10 tasks is weak evidence of invariance. A proper equivalence test (TOST) is not reported.\n- The evidence for \"routing\" (readout concentration) is a 4.5 percentage point improvement in R-squared. This is statistically significant but practically small -- it accounts for an incremental improvement over what an untrained model already partially achieves (0.686).\n- The title-level claim \"training routes, not creates\" is bolder than the evidence warrants. A more defensible version would be: \"training primarily improves information routing with negligible change to distributed representations.\"\n\n**Limitations discussion:** The paper acknowledges single-architecture limitation (appropriate). However, it does not adequately discuss:\n- The untrained control initializes a RANDOM LLM while preserving the frozen encoder. This is an extreme comparison -- the \"untrained\" model has never seen any text or images through its LLM. The claim would be much stronger if comparing early-stage vs. late-stage fine-tuning, or comparing few-shot vs. full fine-tuning.\n- RSA on 10 tasks with 500 frames each -- the statistical power for detecting small effects may be limited.\n- LIBERO is a simulation benchmark with limited visual and motor complexity.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Impact and Broader Significance\n\n**Would this paper influence future VLA research/design?** Partially. The finding that vision encoder pretraining quality matters most is confirmatory of existing trends. The practical implication for action head design (concentrate information into readout tokens) is actionable but incremental. The geometric-functional dissociation finding could influence encoder selection criteria (optimize for geometric alignment, not just decodability), but this needs more development.\n\n**Practical implications:**\n- \"Choose VL-pretrained encoders over vision-only\" -- already standard practice in most VLA designs.\n- \"LLM routing architecture matters\" -- underspecified; what specifically should change?\n- \"Invest in encoder pretraining not LLM fine-tuning\" -- potentially misleading since the paper does not test what happens with MORE fine-tuning data, only the binary trained/untrained contrast.\n\nThe impact is primarily to the interpretability/analysis community rather than practitioners.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Weaknesses and Questions\n\n**Major Weaknesses:**\n\nW1. **Single architecture, single benchmark suite.** OpenVLA is one architecture with one specific design (frozen SigLIP, Llama-2 7B, autoregressive action tokens). Modern VLAs like pi0.5 use flow matching with separate expert pathways. The \"vision encoder dominance\" may not hold for architectures where the action pathway is structurally separated from the VLM pathway. Testing even one additional architecture (even Octo, which is open-source) would substantially strengthen the claims.\n\nW2. **Extreme untrained control.** Comparing a fully fine-tuned model against a randomly-initialized LLM is the weakest possible test of \"does training change representations.\" A more informative comparison would be: base pretrained Llama-2 (language-only pretraining) vs. fine-tuned OpenVLA. The current comparison conflates \"VLA fine-tuning effect\" with \"language pretraining + VLA fine-tuning effect\" in the trained condition, vs. \"nothing\" in the untrained condition. The fact that representations are similar despite this extreme manipulation is LESS surprising than it would be if comparing pretrained vs. fine-tuned -- because the frozen encoder+projector dominate input, both models receive identical inputs and are thus constrained to produce similar early-layer representations.\n\nW3. **Confirmatory of concurrent work.** Grant et al. (2026) already established \"visual pathway dominates\" and \"language encoded but not used\" across 6 architectures with real-world deployment. This paper confirms the same finding at a different analysis level but does not substantially advance the understanding beyond Grant's conclusions. The geometric-functional dissociation is the main differentiator but occupies relatively little of the paper.\n\nW4. **RSA metric limitations.** The RSA metric measures Spearman correlation between distance structures. It is sensitive to the specific distance metric used and the sampling of conditions. The paper reports high variance across tasks (CLIP ranges 0.235-0.783). The reliance on a single geometric metric as the primary evidence, especially when CKA REVERSES the ordering, is methodologically fragile.\n\nW5. **Language instruction finding is trivially expected.** The paper reports language instructions have no effect on representations. But the trained model was fine-tuned on LIBERO-10 where (as Hou 2026 and others documented) each scene uniquely determines the task, making language instructions unnecessary. The model learned to ignore instructions because they were redundant in training. This is not evidence that VLAs generally cannot use language -- it is evidence that this specific model on this specific benchmark does not.\n\n**Minor Weaknesses:**\n\nW6. The p=0.08 for CLIP vs SigLIP+Projector comparison is presented as \"statistically indistinguishable.\" With n=10 tasks, this is simply underpowered. The CI (0.442-0.660 for CLIP) is extremely wide.\n\nW7. The paper does not report what happens at the action head itself -- only the readout token before the action head. The final transformation from readout to discrete action tokens may further modify the picture.\n\nW8. \"Cross-benchmark ratios 0.95-0.97\" -- this sounds like a replication, but LIBERO-Spatial and LIBERO-Goal share the same codebase, similar visual style, and related task structures. True generalization would require a different simulator or real-world data.\n\n**Questions for Authors:**\n\nQ1. What does the RSA look like for a base Llama-2 pretrained model (not fine-tuned for actions, but not randomly initialized either)? This would isolate the contribution of VLA fine-tuning from the contribution of language pretraining.\n\nQ2. The untrained readout R-squared is 0.686 at L32 -- which layers achieve the best untrained image_mean R-squared? The paper reports 0.714 at L0. Why does the very first layer of a random LLM achieve HIGHER R-squared than the projector (0.714 > 0.706)? This seems to contradict the \"degradation\" narrative.\n\nQ3. If we use the action probabilities from the actual action head (not a learned probe), what is the action prediction accuracy of trained vs. untrained models? This grounds the representation-level analysis in actual behavioral differences.\n\nQ4. The CKA ordering reversal (untrained > trained > projector) is described but not adequately explained. Why would an untrained LLM have HIGHER CKA with action structure than a trained one?\n\nQ5. For the CLIP control: CLIP uses a different tokenization (14x14 patch vs SigLIP's 14x14) and different dimensionality. Was the comparison controlled for representation dimensionality?\n\n---\n\n### 6. Score and Confidence\n\n**Overall Score: 4 (Weak Accept)**\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High confidence)**\n\n**Justification:** The paper presents a systematic and methodologically sound empirical analysis with one genuinely novel finding (geometric-functional dissociation) and several confirmatory findings that add representational-level evidence to an emerging picture. The experimental design is careful with appropriate controls (null controls, bootstrap CIs, multiple metrics). However, the novelty is diminished by (a) heavy confirmation of concurrent work (Grant et al. 2026), (b) an extreme untrained control that makes the \"invariance\" finding less surprising, (c) single-architecture scope, and (d) the core \"routing not creating\" framing being a restatement of known fine-tuning dynamics in LLMs. The related work coverage is good for VLA-specific literature but misses important connections to the broader probing/fine-tuning literature. The paper is above the reject threshold due to the geometric-functional dissociation insight and careful experimental methodology, but below a clear accept due to limited novelty and generalizability concerns. The paper would benefit from (1) testing at least one additional architecture, (2) including a base-pretrained LLM control, and (3) more explicitly positioning the \"routing\" finding within the broader fine-tuning literature.\n\n---\n\n### Critical Files for Implementation\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/literature_map.yaml\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/paper_framing.md\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/decisions.yaml\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/writing_plan.yaml\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/memory.md"}],"totalDurationMs":136573,"totalTokens":61400,"totalToolUseCount":10,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8964,"cache_read_input_tokens":48472,"output_tokens":3963,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8964},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3963,"cache_read_input_tokens":48472,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8964,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8964,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":0,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a9e4611bcd0f46cf8","tool_use_id":"toolu_01SfF3XocMDWSbNFdtEZgwW6","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":80818,"tool_uses":24,"duration_ms":190844},"uuid":"422f3b3c-ec71-496d-8e7a-7ffed92d38b6","session_id":"f97ff991-0e8f-42e6-bed2-f413306c2d1e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SfF3XocMDWSbNFdtEZgwW6","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my thorough examination of the project files, experimental code, decisions log, paper framing, writing plan, and literature map, I can now provide a comprehensive peer review.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods and Experiments Review\n\n**Paper:** \"Where Do Actions Come From? A Representational Decomposition of Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\n---\n\n### 1. Methodological Soundness\n\n**Appropriateness of Tools (RSA, CKA, Functional Probes):**\n\nThe combination of RSA, CKA, and functional action probes is well-chosen and mutually complementary. RSA (Kriegeskorte et al., 2008) measures local geometric alignment between representation and action distance structures; CKA (Kornblith et al., 2019) captures global structure preservation; and functional probes (MLP regression) test whether action-relevant information is linearly decodable. The paper correctly identifies and discusses the RSA-CKA dissociation (ordering reversal: CKA shows untrained > trained > projector > DINO, while RSA shows projector > trained > untrained > DINO), which adds methodological depth rather than being a weakness. The authors appropriately scope Claim 2 to \"geometric (RSA) degradation\" rather than generic \"representation degradation.\"\n\nHowever, there are methodological concerns:\n\n1. **RSA dissimilarity metric choice:** The code reveals cosine distance is used for representational dissimilarity, but Euclidean distance or correlation distance are also common. No justification is provided for this choice, and no sensitivity analysis across metrics is reported.\n\n2. **Temporal binning in RSA:** The code divides trajectories into early/mid/late thirds and subsamples to 20 frames for pairwise comparison. This averaging could mask temporal dynamics. The paper reports mean-across-phases RSA but the choice of 3 bins and 20-sample alignment is arbitrary without justification.\n\n3. **Functional probe architecture:** The MLP probe (Linear-ReLU-Linear, 256 hidden units) is a specific capacity choice. The paper does not report sensitivity to probe complexity. The Hewitt & Manning (2019) concern about probe expressivity — which the authors themselves identified during development (D018) — is never formally addressed in the final paper framing. A linear probe baseline for the functional results would strengthen the claim that differences are about information content rather than probe overfitting.\n\n**Experimental Design (2x2 Trained/Untrained x With/No Instruction):**\n\nThe 2x2 factorial design is well-constructed and appropriate. The \"untrained\" control (reinitializing only the LLM weights via `from_config()` while preserving the frozen SigLIP encoder) is a strong design choice that isolates the LLM's contribution. The instruction ablation is further strengthened by the random/shuffled instruction controls (plan_008), which establish a three-level invariance hierarchy (training, instruction presence, instruction content).\n\n**Confounds and Alternative Explanations:**\n\n1. **Critical confound — \"untrained\" is not truly random:** The untrained model reinitializes the LLM (Llama-2 7B) with random weights but keeps the pretrained SigLIP + projector frozen. This means \"untrained\" still has a structured vision pathway. The comparison is actually \"pretrained LLM + fine-tuning\" vs \"random LLM\" — a much larger perturbation than \"before vs after fine-tuning.\" The fact that these produce similar RSA (0.501 vs 0.485) is indeed surprising, but the interpretation \"training does not change representations\" could alternatively mean \"the projector bottleneck constrains representations regardless of downstream weights.\" The authors identified this (D026: \"projector bottleneck mechanism\") but it is unclear whether this is adequately discussed as an alternative explanation versus the stronger \"training = routing\" interpretation.\n\n2. **Aggregation dependence:** The paper acknowledges that results change by aggregation method (image_mean vs last_preaction). For RSA, both converge (~0.50 vs ~0.49), but for functional probes the story flips: projector > LLM image_mean, but LLM last_preaction > projector. This is intellectually honest but makes the \"degradation\" claim aggregation-dependent. The authors handle this well by using image_mean as primary and last_preaction as supplementary.\n\n3. **Copy baseline issue:** During development, a copy baseline (a_{t+1} = a_t) achieved R^2 = 0.97 due to trajectory smoothness, which the authors identified (D012). Action-delta probes were introduced to address this, but the final paper's primary functional metric remains raw action R^2. The extent to which the R^2 ~ 0.70 values are inflated by temporal autocorrelation is not discussed.\n\n**Statistical Framework:**\n\nThe bootstrap CI approach (10,000 resamples with fixed seed 42) and permutation tests are appropriate for the non-parametric comparisons made. The code shows bootstrap over per-task RSA values (n=10 tasks), which is reasonable but yields wide CIs given the small number of tasks. The p-values reported (e.g., training effect p=0.20) are derived from bootstrap null distributions, which is appropriate. However:\n\n- The fixed random seed (42) in bootstrap is acceptable for reproducibility but should be mentioned.\n- With only 10 tasks, the bootstrap on per-task means has limited resolution. The CI width ([−0.031, +0.064] for training effect) is large enough that a true small effect could be missed (low statistical power for small effects).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n\n**Are experiments sufficient to support the three claims?**\n\n**Claim 1 (Vision Encoder Dominance):** Strongly supported. SigLIP+Projector RSA = 0.615 vs Full VLA = 0.501 vs DINO = 0.388 provides clear ordering. The CLIP ViT-L control (RSA = 0.557) confirms VL pretraining generality. The geometric-functional dissociation (DINO R^2 = 0.712 approximately equal to Projector R^2 = 0.706, p=0.22, but DINO RSA = 0.388 << Projector RSA = 0.615) is a genuinely novel and interesting finding.\n\n**Claim 2 (LLM Redistributes):** Moderately supported. The redistribution narrative — LLM degrades distributed image tokens (R^2: 0.706 to 0.683) but concentrates into readout (R^2 = 0.731) — is coherent. The training-dependence of readout concentration (trained 0.731 vs untrained 0.686, p<0.0001) is well-established statistically. However, the per-layer degradation curve being non-monotonic and the CKA showing monotonic increase complicates the narrative. The claim requires careful scoping.\n\n**Claim 3 (Training Routes, Not Creates):** Moderately supported. The null result on distributed RSA (p=0.20) combined with the significant readout concentration gap (p<0.0001) tells a clean story. But null results are inherently harder to establish — absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. The power to detect a true small training effect (say, delta = 0.03) with 10 tasks is limited.\n\n**Sample Sizes:**\n\n- 50 demos x 10 tasks = 500 trajectories per benchmark is reasonable for the RSA/probing analyses conducted.\n- For bootstrap CIs, the effective sample size is 10 (number of tasks), which is the key limitation. Per-task RSA variability is high (CLIP ranges 0.235-0.783 across tasks), so task-level replication is the binding constraint.\n\n**CLIP Control Experiment:**\n\nThe CLIP ViT-L/14 control (RSA = 0.557) is convincing for establishing that VL pretraining (not SigLIP specifically) drives geometric alignment. However, the CLIP action probe R^2 is noted as \"pending\" in the experiment plan, which is a gap — we cannot confirm the geometric-functional dissociation holds for CLIP as well.\n\n**LIBERO-Spatial Replication:**\n\nAccording to the paper summary, LIBERO-Spatial replication achieves cross-benchmark ratios of 0.95-0.97 with the same pattern. However, from the project files, plan_010 (LIBERO-Spatial) has status \"planned\" and was never actually executed (\"时间允许才做\"). The experiment plan shows it as P2 priority with no registry_id. **This appears to be a significant discrepancy between what the paper claims and what was actually run.** If the paper reports LIBERO-Spatial results (Tables 4-7), I need clarification on when these were obtained — the project infrastructure suggests they may not have been completed.\n\n**UPDATE:** Re-examining the paper summary more carefully, it mentions \"LIBERO-Spatial\" results. But the project's experiment_plan.yaml shows plan_010 as \"planned\" with no results. The original MVP experiments (mvp_001 through mvp_controlb) were run on LIBERO-10 (which contains spatial tasks), and the main RSA/probe analyses were on LIBERO-Goal. It is possible that \"LIBERO-Spatial\" refers to a subset or that these results are extrapolated. This needs careful verification.\n\n**Missing Experiments and Ablations:**\n\n1. **No downstream task performance correlation.** The paper measures representational metrics but never connects them to actual manipulation success rates. A reader naturally asks: does higher RSA predict better task completion? This was identified in D030 but never addressed beyond probing.\n\n2. **No probe complexity ablation.** Linear vs 1-layer MLP vs 2-layer MLP would clarify whether differences are about information presence or probe capacity.\n\n3. **No multi-seed robustness for the untrained model.** Only one random initialization of the LLM is tested. Different random seeds might yield different RSA values.\n\n4. **CLIP action probe R^2 missing.** The geometric-functional dissociation is established for DINO but not confirmed for CLIP.\n\n5. **No causal intervention.** All analyses are correlational (probing/RSA). An activation patching or representation surgery experiment would elevate the claims from observational to causal.\n\n**Baselines:**\n\nDINOv2 as a vision-only baseline is appropriate. The untrained VLA (random LLM) is a strong control. The projector output as the \"pre-LLM\" representation is the natural intermediate. The random/shuffled/empty instruction conditions (plan_008) are creative and thorough.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Clarity of Presentation\n\nBased on the writing plan, the paper is structured logically with clear subsections per claim. Tables 1-7 cover the main results systematically. The planned figures (architecture diagram, bar charts, per-layer curves, 2x2 heatmap) are appropriate.\n\n**Potential clarity issues:**\n\n- The distinction between \"image_mean\" and \"last_preaction\" aggregation is crucial but easily confusing. The paper needs very clear notation.\n- The RSA-CKA dissociation (Section 3.5) could confuse readers if not carefully framed. It should be positioned as \"what kind of geometric property does LLM processing affect?\" rather than appearing to undermine the main claims.\n- The relationship between \"LIBERO-10\" (fine-tuning data) and \"LIBERO-Goal\" (primary analysis benchmark, OOD for the trained model) needs explicit discussion. The trained model was fine-tuned on LIBERO-10 but analyzed on LIBERO-Goal — meaning the \"trained\" model is actually OOD on the analysis benchmark.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Weaknesses and Questions\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n\n**W1 (Major): Single architecture, single model scale.** All claims are established on OpenVLA-7B only. The paper title (\"Vision-Language-Action Models\" plural) overclaims relative to evidence from one model. Flow-matching VLAs (pi0), diffusion-based VLAs (Octo), or larger autoregressive VLAs (RT-2) may behave differently. The paper must scope claims precisely to \"OpenVLA-class autoregressive VLAs.\"\n\n**W2 (Major): Possible LIBERO-Spatial fabrication concern.** The paper summary describes Tables 4-7 with LIBERO-Spatial cross-benchmark results (ratios 0.95-0.97), but the project infrastructure shows plan_010 as \"planned\" and never executed. If these results are claimed in the paper without having been run, this is a critical integrity issue. If they were run through a different mechanism, this needs clarification.\n\n**W3 (Moderate): Null result interpretation.** The claim \"training does not change distributed representation structure\" (Claim 3) rests on a non-significant difference (p=0.20). With n=10 tasks and observed delta=0.021, the statistical power to detect a true small effect is very low. The paper should report a power analysis or equivalence test (TOST) rather than interpreting non-significance as evidence for the null.\n\n**W4 (Moderate): Trained model is OOD on analysis benchmark.** The trained model was fine-tuned on LIBERO-10 but representations are analyzed on LIBERO-Goal (confirmed in D017). This means the \"trained\" model has never seen LIBERO-Goal scenes during fine-tuning. The comparison is therefore \"pretrained + LIBERO-10 fine-tuned, tested OOD on LIBERO-Goal\" vs \"random LLM on LIBERO-Goal.\" The training signal from LIBERO-10 may simply not transfer to LIBERO-Goal representations, which would explain the null training effect without supporting the general claim that \"training does not create.\"\n\n**W5 (Minor): Copy baseline and autocorrelation.** Action R^2 values around 0.70 are presented as meaningful, but given that a simple copy baseline achieves R^2 = 0.97 on raw actions, the absolute R^2 scale is compressed. The meaningful signal exists in the 0.03-0.05 range of differences between conditions, which is small in absolute terms.\n\n**W6 (Minor): Projector is not \"vision encoder only.\"** The \"SigLIP+Projector\" representation passes through a learned MLP projector that was trained during VLA pre-training. Calling this \"vision encoder dominance\" slightly overclaims — it is \"vision encoder + alignment projector\" dominance. The projector's contribution versus raw SigLIP output is not isolated.\n\n**Questions for Authors:**\n\n**Q1:** Were the LIBERO-Spatial results (Tables 4-7) actually computed? If so, through which experiment run? The project infrastructure does not show a completed plan_010.\n\n**Q2:** Have you considered running a Two One-Sided Tests (TOST) equivalence procedure for Claim 3, to affirmatively establish equivalence within a pre-specified margin (e.g., delta < 0.05) rather than relying on non-significance?\n\n**Q3:** What is the R^2 of a linear probe (no hidden layer) at the projector and best LLM layer? This would address the Hewitt & Manning (2019) concern about probe complexity.\n\n**Q4:** Would the trained model show different results if analyzed on LIBERO-10 (in-distribution) rather than LIBERO-Goal (OOD)? The training effect null result might be specific to OOD evaluation.\n\n**Q5:** How sensitive are the RSA results to the number of samples per trajectory pair (currently fixed at n_sample=20 in the code)?\n\n**Q6:** The CKA ordering reversal (untrained > trained) is surprising. Do you have an interpretation for why a random LLM would preserve more global structure than a trained one?\n\n---\n\n### 5. Score and Confidence\n\n**Overall Score: 4 (Weak Accept)**\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High — I have expertise in representation analysis methods and VLA systems)**\n\n**Justification:**\n\nThe paper presents a systematic and methodologically sound representational analysis of OpenVLA with three coherent findings. The geometric-functional dissociation (Claim 1) and the redistribution mechanism (Claim 2) are genuinely novel contributions that advance understanding of VLA internals. The statistical framework is appropriate, and the multiple control conditions (untrained LLM, DINO baseline, instruction ablation, random instructions) demonstrate thorough experimental thinking.\n\nHowever, the paper has non-trivial weaknesses: (1) single-architecture scope limits generalizability, (2) the cross-benchmark replication claim may not be supported by actual experiments, (3) the null result in Claim 3 lacks formal equivalence testing, and (4) the OOD confound between fine-tuning data and analysis benchmark weakens the \"training does not create\" interpretation. The contribution is primarily descriptive/observational rather than causal, and the practical implications (encoder selection matters more than fine-tuning) are partially anticipated by Grant et al. (2026) at the behavioral level.\n\nOn balance, the methodological rigor, novel findings (especially geometric-functional dissociation and training-dependent readout concentration), and systematic approach merit acceptance, but the scope limitations and the potential LIBERO-Spatial verification gap prevent a stronger recommendation.\n\n---\n\n### Critical Files for Implementation\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/artifacts/rsa_analysis.py\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/paper_framing.md\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/writing_plan.yaml\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/decisions.yaml\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/experiment_plan.yaml"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f97ff991-0e8f-42e6-bed2-f413306c2d1e","uuid":"890026b3-92ce-4ad8-ad2c-e08484603e41","timestamp":"2026-05-16T01:44:55.147Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. Evaluate the paper below using a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Where Do Actions Come From? A Representational Decomposition of Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\nThe paper performs a systematic representational analysis of OpenVLA, decomposing contributions from the frozen vision encoder (SigLIP), projector, and LLM backbone. Using RSA, CKA, and functional action probes across two LIBERO benchmarks and three vision encoders (SigLIP, CLIP ViT-L, DINOv2), they establish three findings:\n\n1. **VL-pretrained encoder dominance**: SigLIP+Projector achieves highest RSA (ρ=0.615), CLIP ViT-L is statistically indistinguishable (ρ=0.553, p=0.08), DINO significantly lower (ρ=0.388). But all encoders achieve comparable action probe R²≈0.70 — geometric-functional dissociation.\n\n2. **LLM redistributes, training-dependently**: RSA drops from projector (0.615) to LLM image_mean (0.501), but trained readout token achieves R²=0.731 > projector 0.706. Untrained readout R²=0.686 < projector — concentration is learned.\n\n3. **Training routes, not creates**: Trained vs untrained RSA difference is non-significant (0.501 vs 0.485, p=0.20). But readout concentration gap is large (ΔR²=0.045, p<0.0001). Language instructions have no effect.\n\nAll findings replicate on LIBERO-Spatial with cross-benchmark ratios 0.95–0.97.\n\n**Key Tables:**\n- Table 1: Vision encoder RSA comparison (SigLIP 0.615, CLIP 0.553, VLA 0.501, DINO 0.388; all R²≈0.70)\n- Table 2: Pairwise bootstrap tests (CLIP vs SigLIP p=0.08 NS; CLIP vs DINO p=0.001 sig)\n- Table 3: 2×2 RSA matrix (all values in [0.48, 0.50], training Δ=0.016 NS, instruction Δ≈0)\n- Table 4: Cross-benchmark RSA (ratios 0.955–0.965)\n- Table 5: 2×2 RSA matrix LIBERO-Spatial (same pattern)\n- Table 6: Functional probe R² cross-benchmark (all Δ &lt; 0.021)\n- Table 7: Bootstrap tests LIBERO-Spatial (training RSA NS, readout concentration sig)\n\nCKA–RSA dissociation: CKA ordering reverses RSA ordering (untrained > trained > projector > DINO).\n\n## Experiment Design Context\n\nThe imagined experiment plan includes:\n1. **CLIP-ViT Pretraining Control** — use CLIP ViT-L/14 to test VL-pretraining generality beyond SigLIP. Expected: CLIP RSA=0.553, not significantly different from SigLIP (p=0.08), significantly > DINO (p=0.001).\n2. **LIBERO-Spatial Cross-Benchmark Validation** — full replication of all analyses on LIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks). Expected: same ordering, ratios ~0.95–0.97.\n\nBoth experiments appear fully covered in the paper.\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions and provide detailed comments:\n\n### 1. Methodological Soundness\n- Are RSA, CKA, and functional probes appropriate tools for the claims made?\n- Is the experimental design (trained/untrained × with/no instruction) well-constructed?\n- Are there confounds or alternative explanations not addressed?\n- Is the statistical framework (bootstrap CIs, permutation tests) appropriate and correctly applied?\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n- Are the experiments sufficient to support the three claims?\n- Sample sizes (50 demos × 10 tasks = 500 per benchmark) — adequate?\n- Is the CLIP control experiment convincing for generalizing beyond SigLIP?\n- Is LIBERO-Spatial replication sufficient for \"cross-benchmark\" generalization claims?\n- Are there missing experiments or ablations?\n- Are baselines appropriate?\n\n### 3. Clarity of Presentation\n- Are the methods clearly described and reproducible?\n- Are tables and figures well-organized?\n\n### 4. Weaknesses and Questions\n- List specific weaknesses\n- Raise specific questions for the authors\n\n### 5. Score and Confidence\n- Overall score (1-6)\n- Confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n- Brief justification\n\nOutput a complete structured review. Do NOT write any code or make edits. This is pure analysis.\n","agentId":"a9e4611bcd0f46cf8","agentType":"Plan","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my thorough examination of the project files, experimental code, decisions log, paper framing, writing plan, and literature map, I can now provide a comprehensive peer review.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods and Experiments Review\n\n**Paper:** \"Where Do Actions Come From? A Representational Decomposition of Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\n---\n\n### 1. Methodological Soundness\n\n**Appropriateness of Tools (RSA, CKA, Functional Probes):**\n\nThe combination of RSA, CKA, and functional action probes is well-chosen and mutually complementary. RSA (Kriegeskorte et al., 2008) measures local geometric alignment between representation and action distance structures; CKA (Kornblith et al., 2019) captures global structure preservation; and functional probes (MLP regression) test whether action-relevant information is linearly decodable. The paper correctly identifies and discusses the RSA-CKA dissociation (ordering reversal: CKA shows untrained > trained > projector > DINO, while RSA shows projector > trained > untrained > DINO), which adds methodological depth rather than being a weakness. The authors appropriately scope Claim 2 to \"geometric (RSA) degradation\" rather than generic \"representation degradation.\"\n\nHowever, there are methodological concerns:\n\n1. **RSA dissimilarity metric choice:** The code reveals cosine distance is used for representational dissimilarity, but Euclidean distance or correlation distance are also common. No justification is provided for this choice, and no sensitivity analysis across metrics is reported.\n\n2. **Temporal binning in RSA:** The code divides trajectories into early/mid/late thirds and subsamples to 20 frames for pairwise comparison. This averaging could mask temporal dynamics. The paper reports mean-across-phases RSA but the choice of 3 bins and 20-sample alignment is arbitrary without justification.\n\n3. **Functional probe architecture:** The MLP probe (Linear-ReLU-Linear, 256 hidden units) is a specific capacity choice. The paper does not report sensitivity to probe complexity. The Hewitt & Manning (2019) concern about probe expressivity — which the authors themselves identified during development (D018) — is never formally addressed in the final paper framing. A linear probe baseline for the functional results would strengthen the claim that differences are about information content rather than probe overfitting.\n\n**Experimental Design (2x2 Trained/Untrained x With/No Instruction):**\n\nThe 2x2 factorial design is well-constructed and appropriate. The \"untrained\" control (reinitializing only the LLM weights via `from_config()` while preserving the frozen SigLIP encoder) is a strong design choice that isolates the LLM's contribution. The instruction ablation is further strengthened by the random/shuffled instruction controls (plan_008), which establish a three-level invariance hierarchy (training, instruction presence, instruction content).\n\n**Confounds and Alternative Explanations:**\n\n1. **Critical confound — \"untrained\" is not truly random:** The untrained model reinitializes the LLM (Llama-2 7B) with random weights but keeps the pretrained SigLIP + projector frozen. This means \"untrained\" still has a structured vision pathway. The comparison is actually \"pretrained LLM + fine-tuning\" vs \"random LLM\" — a much larger perturbation than \"before vs after fine-tuning.\" The fact that these produce similar RSA (0.501 vs 0.485) is indeed surprising, but the interpretation \"training does not change representations\" could alternatively mean \"the projector bottleneck constrains representations regardless of downstream weights.\" The authors identified this (D026: \"projector bottleneck mechanism\") but it is unclear whether this is adequately discussed as an alternative explanation versus the stronger \"training = routing\" interpretation.\n\n2. **Aggregation dependence:** The paper acknowledges that results change by aggregation method (image_mean vs last_preaction). For RSA, both converge (~0.50 vs ~0.49), but for functional probes the story flips: projector > LLM image_mean, but LLM last_preaction > projector. This is intellectually honest but makes the \"degradation\" claim aggregation-dependent. The authors handle this well by using image_mean as primary and last_preaction as supplementary.\n\n3. **Copy baseline issue:** During development, a copy baseline (a_{t+1} = a_t) achieved R^2 = 0.97 due to trajectory smoothness, which the authors identified (D012). Action-delta probes were introduced to address this, but the final paper's primary functional metric remains raw action R^2. The extent to which the R^2 ~ 0.70 values are inflated by temporal autocorrelation is not discussed.\n\n**Statistical Framework:**\n\nThe bootstrap CI approach (10,000 resamples with fixed seed 42) and permutation tests are appropriate for the non-parametric comparisons made. The code shows bootstrap over per-task RSA values (n=10 tasks), which is reasonable but yields wide CIs given the small number of tasks. The p-values reported (e.g., training effect p=0.20) are derived from bootstrap null distributions, which is appropriate. However:\n\n- The fixed random seed (42) in bootstrap is acceptable for reproducibility but should be mentioned.\n- With only 10 tasks, the bootstrap on per-task means has limited resolution. The CI width ([−0.031, +0.064] for training effect) is large enough that a true small effect could be missed (low statistical power for small effects).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n\n**Are experiments sufficient to support the three claims?**\n\n**Claim 1 (Vision Encoder Dominance):** Strongly supported. SigLIP+Projector RSA = 0.615 vs Full VLA = 0.501 vs DINO = 0.388 provides clear ordering. The CLIP ViT-L control (RSA = 0.557) confirms VL pretraining generality. The geometric-functional dissociation (DINO R^2 = 0.712 approximately equal to Projector R^2 = 0.706, p=0.22, but DINO RSA = 0.388 << Projector RSA = 0.615) is a genuinely novel and interesting finding.\n\n**Claim 2 (LLM Redistributes):** Moderately supported. The redistribution narrative — LLM degrades distributed image tokens (R^2: 0.706 to 0.683) but concentrates into readout (R^2 = 0.731) — is coherent. The training-dependence of readout concentration (trained 0.731 vs untrained 0.686, p<0.0001) is well-established statistically. However, the per-layer degradation curve being non-monotonic and the CKA showing monotonic increase complicates the narrative. The claim requires careful scoping.\n\n**Claim 3 (Training Routes, Not Creates):** Moderately supported. The null result on distributed RSA (p=0.20) combined with the significant readout concentration gap (p<0.0001) tells a clean story. But null results are inherently harder to establish — absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. The power to detect a true small training effect (say, delta = 0.03) with 10 tasks is limited.\n\n**Sample Sizes:**\n\n- 50 demos x 10 tasks = 500 trajectories per benchmark is reasonable for the RSA/probing analyses conducted.\n- For bootstrap CIs, the effective sample size is 10 (number of tasks), which is the key limitation. Per-task RSA variability is high (CLIP ranges 0.235-0.783 across tasks), so task-level replication is the binding constraint.\n\n**CLIP Control Experiment:**\n\nThe CLIP ViT-L/14 control (RSA = 0.557) is convincing for establishing that VL pretraining (not SigLIP specifically) drives geometric alignment. However, the CLIP action probe R^2 is noted as \"pending\" in the experiment plan, which is a gap — we cannot confirm the geometric-functional dissociation holds for CLIP as well.\n\n**LIBERO-Spatial Replication:**\n\nAccording to the paper summary, LIBERO-Spatial replication achieves cross-benchmark ratios of 0.95-0.97 with the same pattern. However, from the project files, plan_010 (LIBERO-Spatial) has status \"planned\" and was never actually executed (\"时间允许才做\"). The experiment plan shows it as P2 priority with no registry_id. **This appears to be a significant discrepancy between what the paper claims and what was actually run.** If the paper reports LIBERO-Spatial results (Tables 4-7), I need clarification on when these were obtained — the project infrastructure suggests they may not have been completed.\n\n**UPDATE:** Re-examining the paper summary more carefully, it mentions \"LIBERO-Spatial\" results. But the project's experiment_plan.yaml shows plan_010 as \"planned\" with no results. The original MVP experiments (mvp_001 through mvp_controlb) were run on LIBERO-10 (which contains spatial tasks), and the main RSA/probe analyses were on LIBERO-Goal. It is possible that \"LIBERO-Spatial\" refers to a subset or that these results are extrapolated. This needs careful verification.\n\n**Missing Experiments and Ablations:**\n\n1. **No downstream task performance correlation.** The paper measures representational metrics but never connects them to actual manipulation success rates. A reader naturally asks: does higher RSA predict better task completion? This was identified in D030 but never addressed beyond probing.\n\n2. **No probe complexity ablation.** Linear vs 1-layer MLP vs 2-layer MLP would clarify whether differences are about information presence or probe capacity.\n\n3. **No multi-seed robustness for the untrained model.** Only one random initialization of the LLM is tested. Different random seeds might yield different RSA values.\n\n4. **CLIP action probe R^2 missing.** The geometric-functional dissociation is established for DINO but not confirmed for CLIP.\n\n5. **No causal intervention.** All analyses are correlational (probing/RSA). An activation patching or representation surgery experiment would elevate the claims from observational to causal.\n\n**Baselines:**\n\nDINOv2 as a vision-only baseline is appropriate. The untrained VLA (random LLM) is a strong control. The projector output as the \"pre-LLM\" representation is the natural intermediate. The random/shuffled/empty instruction conditions (plan_008) are creative and thorough.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Clarity of Presentation\n\nBased on the writing plan, the paper is structured logically with clear subsections per claim. Tables 1-7 cover the main results systematically. The planned figures (architecture diagram, bar charts, per-layer curves, 2x2 heatmap) are appropriate.\n\n**Potential clarity issues:**\n\n- The distinction between \"image_mean\" and \"last_preaction\" aggregation is crucial but easily confusing. The paper needs very clear notation.\n- The RSA-CKA dissociation (Section 3.5) could confuse readers if not carefully framed. It should be positioned as \"what kind of geometric property does LLM processing affect?\" rather than appearing to undermine the main claims.\n- The relationship between \"LIBERO-10\" (fine-tuning data) and \"LIBERO-Goal\" (primary analysis benchmark, OOD for the trained model) needs explicit discussion. The trained model was fine-tuned on LIBERO-10 but analyzed on LIBERO-Goal — meaning the \"trained\" model is actually OOD on the analysis benchmark.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Weaknesses and Questions\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n\n**W1 (Major): Single architecture, single model scale.** All claims are established on OpenVLA-7B only. The paper title (\"Vision-Language-Action Models\" plural) overclaims relative to evidence from one model. Flow-matching VLAs (pi0), diffusion-based VLAs (Octo), or larger autoregressive VLAs (RT-2) may behave differently. The paper must scope claims precisely to \"OpenVLA-class autoregressive VLAs.\"\n\n**W2 (Major): Possible LIBERO-Spatial fabrication concern.** The paper summary describes Tables 4-7 with LIBERO-Spatial cross-benchmark results (ratios 0.95-0.97), but the project infrastructure shows plan_010 as \"planned\" and never executed. If these results are claimed in the paper without having been run, this is a critical integrity issue. If they were run through a different mechanism, this needs clarification.\n\n**W3 (Moderate): Null result interpretation.** The claim \"training does not change distributed representation structure\" (Claim 3) rests on a non-significant difference (p=0.20). With n=10 tasks and observed delta=0.021, the statistical power to detect a true small effect is very low. The paper should report a power analysis or equivalence test (TOST) rather than interpreting non-significance as evidence for the null.\n\n**W4 (Moderate): Trained model is OOD on analysis benchmark.** The trained model was fine-tuned on LIBERO-10 but representations are analyzed on LIBERO-Goal (confirmed in D017). This means the \"trained\" model has never seen LIBERO-Goal scenes during fine-tuning. The comparison is therefore \"pretrained + LIBERO-10 fine-tuned, tested OOD on LIBERO-Goal\" vs \"random LLM on LIBERO-Goal.\" The training signal from LIBERO-10 may simply not transfer to LIBERO-Goal representations, which would explain the null training effect without supporting the general claim that \"training does not create.\"\n\n**W5 (Minor): Copy baseline and autocorrelation.** Action R^2 values around 0.70 are presented as meaningful, but given that a simple copy baseline achieves R^2 = 0.97 on raw actions, the absolute R^2 scale is compressed. The meaningful signal exists in the 0.03-0.05 range of differences between conditions, which is small in absolute terms.\n\n**W6 (Minor): Projector is not \"vision encoder only.\"** The \"SigLIP+Projector\" representation passes through a learned MLP projector that was trained during VLA pre-training. Calling this \"vision encoder dominance\" slightly overclaims — it is \"vision encoder + alignment projector\" dominance. The projector's contribution versus raw SigLIP output is not isolated.\n\n**Questions for Authors:**\n\n**Q1:** Were the LIBERO-Spatial results (Tables 4-7) actually computed? If so, through which experiment run? The project infrastructure does not show a completed plan_010.\n\n**Q2:** Have you considered running a Two One-Sided Tests (TOST) equivalence procedure for Claim 3, to affirmatively establish equivalence within a pre-specified margin (e.g., delta < 0.05) rather than relying on non-significance?\n\n**Q3:** What is the R^2 of a linear probe (no hidden layer) at the projector and best LLM layer? This would address the Hewitt & Manning (2019) concern about probe complexity.\n\n**Q4:** Would the trained model show different results if analyzed on LIBERO-10 (in-distribution) rather than LIBERO-Goal (OOD)? The training effect null result might be specific to OOD evaluation.\n\n**Q5:** How sensitive are the RSA results to the number of samples per trajectory pair (currently fixed at n_sample=20 in the code)?\n\n**Q6:** The CKA ordering reversal (untrained > trained) is surprising. Do you have an interpretation for why a random LLM would preserve more global structure than a trained one?\n\n---\n\n### 5. Score and Confidence\n\n**Overall Score: 4 (Weak Accept)**\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High — I have expertise in representation analysis methods and VLA systems)**\n\n**Justification:**\n\nThe paper presents a systematic and methodologically sound representational analysis of OpenVLA with three coherent findings. The geometric-functional dissociation (Claim 1) and the redistribution mechanism (Claim 2) are genuinely novel contributions that advance understanding of VLA internals. The statistical framework is appropriate, and the multiple control conditions (untrained LLM, DINO baseline, instruction ablation, random instructions) demonstrate thorough experimental thinking.\n\nHowever, the paper has non-trivial weaknesses: (1) single-architecture scope limits generalizability, (2) the cross-benchmark replication claim may not be supported by actual experiments, (3) the null result in Claim 3 lacks formal equivalence testing, and (4) the OOD confound between fine-tuning data and analysis benchmark weakens the \"training does not create\" interpretation. The contribution is primarily descriptive/observational rather than causal, and the practical implications (encoder selection matters more than fine-tuning) are partially anticipated by Grant et al. (2026) at the behavioral level.\n\nOn balance, the methodological rigor, novel findings (especially geometric-functional dissociation and training-dependent readout concentration), and systematic approach merit acceptance, but the scope limitations and the potential LIBERO-Spatial verification gap prevent a stronger recommendation.\n\n---\n\n### Critical Files for Implementation\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/artifacts/rsa_analysis.py\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/paper_framing.md\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/writing_plan.yaml\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/decisions.yaml\n- /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/experiment_plan.yaml"}],"totalDurationMs":190845,"totalTokens":84048,"totalToolUseCount":24,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":500,"cache_read_input_tokens":79267,"output_tokens":4278,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":500},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":4278,"cache_read_input_tokens":79267,"cache_creation_input_tokens":500,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":500,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":2,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":14}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Ty5oxgnP8yN8hWmX8XsS24","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both reviewers have returned their independent assessments. Now I'll conduct the full roundtable: Step 2 (cross-share), Step 3 (converge), and Step 4 (synthesize and submit).\n\n## Roundtable Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n\n**R1 → R2 key points to consider:**\n- R1 flags a potential LIBERO-Spatial fabrication concern (plan_010 shown as \"planned\" in project infrastructure but results reported in paper). R2 should weigh this.\n- R1 notes the OOD confound: trained model was fine-tuned on LIBERO-10 but analyzed on LIBERO-Goal. This weakens Claim 3's \"training does not create\" interpretation.\n- R1 identifies the copy baseline issue (R²=0.97 for a_t+1=a_t) compressing the meaningful signal range.\n- R1 raises the probe complexity concern (Hewitt & Manning 2019) — no linear probe baseline reported.\n\n**R2 → R1 key points to consider:**\n- R2 emphasizes the extreme nature of the \"untrained\" control (random LLM, not base Llama-2). This makes the invariance finding less surprising than presented.\n- R2 notes significant missing related work: Aghajanyan et al. (2021) on intrinsic dimensionality, Voita et al. (2019) on attention routing, Huh et al. (2024) Platonic Representation Hypothesis.\n- R2 flags that the language instruction null result is trivially expected given LIBERO's redundant instruction design.\n- R2 notes CKA ordering reversal is inadequately explained and potentially undermines the narrative.\n\n## Roundtable Step 3: Convergence\n\n**Points of agreement (both reviewers):**\n1. **Score: Both 4 (Weak Accept)** — consistent assessment.\n2. **Geometric-functional dissociation is the most novel finding** — both reviewers highlight this as genuinely interesting.\n3. **Single-architecture limitation is a major weakness** — both flag that claims about \"VLAs\" (plural) from one model are overclaimed.\n4. **Claim 3 (\"routing not creating\") is the weakest claim** — R1 cites lack of equivalence testing (TOST), R2 cites the extreme untrained control making the null result unsurprising.\n5. **Statistical power concerns** with n=10 tasks for bootstrap inference.\n6. **Confirmatory nature** — findings partially anticipated by Grant et al. (2026) and others.\n\n**Points of productive disagreement:**\n- R1 is more concerned about the LIBERO-Spatial data integrity question; R2 treats cross-benchmark replication as weak regardless (same simulator, similar tasks).\n- R2 places more weight on missing related work connections (fine-tuning literature in NLP); R1 focuses more on missing experimental controls (linear probes, multi-seed).\n- Both concerns are valid and complementary rather than contradictory.\n\n**Consensus resolution:**\n- Overall score converges at **4 (Weak Accept)** — above reject threshold due to methodological rigor and the geometric-functional dissociation insight, but below clear accept due to limited novelty, single-architecture scope, and the weakest claim (Claim 3) resting on a null result with low power.\n- The paper would need at minimum one additional VLA architecture and a proper equivalence test to reach Accept.\n\n## Step 4: Synthesized Report\n\nNow submitting the consolidated review report."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11714,"cache_read_input_tokens":52702,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11714},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f97ff991-0e8f-42e6-bed2-f413306c2d1e","uuid":"8cb605b4-0c99-497f-85c5-4c480a4ecb02"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Ty5oxgnP8yN8hWmX8XsS24","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016rpnNWo5QhC133Y3vEfURU","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: \"Where Do Actions Come From?\"\n\n**dream_id:** 20260516T011810 | **review_mode:** full | **reviewers:** R1 (Methods+Experiments), R2 (Novelty+Related Work)\n\n---\n\n## Consolidated Assessment\n\n| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Consensus |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Overall Score | 4 (Weak Accept) | 4 (Weak Accept) | **4 (Weak Accept)** |\n| Confidence | 4/5 | 4/5 | 4/5 |\n\n**One-line verdict:** A methodologically rigorous empirical analysis with one genuinely novel finding (geometric–functional dissociation) but limited by single-architecture scope, partially confirmatory nature relative to concurrent work, and weak evidence for the boldest claim (Claim 3).\n\n---\n\n## Summary of the Paper\n\nThe paper decomposes the representational structure of OpenVLA into contributions from the frozen vision encoder (SigLIP), learned projector, and fine-tuned LLM backbone. Using RSA, CKA, and functional action probes across two LIBERO benchmarks and three standalone vision encoders (SigLIP, CLIP ViT-L, DINOv2), it establishes three findings:\n\n1. **VL-pretrained encoder dominance:** SigLIP+Projector achieves highest RSA with action structure (ρ=0.615); CLIP ViT-L is statistically indistinguishable (ρ=0.553, p=0.08); DINO significantly lower (ρ=0.388). Yet all encoders carry comparable action information (R²≈0.70).\n2. **LLM redistributes, training-dependently:** RSA degrades from projector to LLM image tokens (0.615→0.501), but trained readout token exceeds the projector (R²=0.731 vs 0.706). Untrained readout cannot achieve this (R²=0.686).\n3. **Training routes, not creates:** Distributed RSA is training-invariant (0.501 vs 0.485, p=0.20) while readout concentration is training-dependent (ΔR²=0.045, p<0.0001). Language instructions affect neither.\n\nAll findings reportedly replicate on LIBERO-Spatial (cross-benchmark ratios 0.95–0.97).\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n### S1. Geometric–Functional Dissociation (Both Reviewers — Major Strength)\nThe most genuinely novel finding. DINO achieves action probe R² comparable to SigLIP+Projector (0.712 vs 0.706, p=0.22) yet has vastly lower RSA (0.388 vs 0.615). This clean separation between \"information presence\" and \"geometric alignment with action structure\" has not been explicitly demonstrated in the VLA domain. It reframes the role of VL pretraining as contributing geometric organization, not information content — a conceptually crisp and practically relevant insight.\n\n### S2. Rigorous Experimental Design (R1)\nThe 2×2 factorial design ({trained, untrained} × {with, no instruction}) with additional random/shuffled/empty instruction controls is thorough. The untrained control (reinitializing LLM weights while preserving frozen encoder+projector) cleanly isolates the LLM's contribution. Bootstrap CIs (10,000 resamples) and permutation-based p-values are appropriate for the non-parametric comparisons.\n\n### S3. Complementary Metrics (Both Reviewers)\nThe RSA–CKA–probe troika captures distinct aspects of representation structure (local geometry, global structure, functional information). The CKA–RSA dissociation (ordering reversal) is intellectually honest and adds depth: the LLM degrades local distance alignment while preserving or improving global structural correspondence.\n\n### S4. CLIP Pretraining Control (R1)\nThe CLIP ViT-L/14 control experiment effectively generalizes Claim 1 from \"SigLIP dominance\" to \"VL-pretrained encoder dominance,\" establishing a three-tier structure: VL-pretrained (ρ∼0.55–0.62) >> VLA backbone (ρ∼0.50) >> self-supervised (ρ∼0.39).\n\n### S5. Clear Practical Implications (R2)\nThe paper provides representational-level grounding for the emerging practical consensus that vision encoder quality matters more than LLM scale for VLA performance.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### W1. Single Architecture, Single Model Scale (Both — Major)\nAll claims are established on OpenVLA-7B only. The paper title (\"Vision-Language-Action Models\" plural) overclaims relative to evidence from one model. Modern VLAs differ architecturally: pi0/pi0.5 use flow matching with separate expert pathways, Octo uses diffusion-based action heads, RT-2-X uses different scales. The \"vision encoder dominance\" may not hold for architectures where the action pathway is structurally separated from the VLM pathway. Testing even one additional architecture (Octo is open-source) would substantially strengthen the claims.\n\n**Recommendation:** Scope claims precisely to \"OpenVLA-class autoregressive VLAs\" or test at least one additional architecture.\n\n### W2. Extreme \"Untrained\" Control (R2 — Major)\nThe \"untrained\" model uses a randomly-initialized LLM, not a base pretrained Llama-2. This is an extreme comparison that conflates \"VLA fine-tuning effect\" with \"language pretraining + VLA fine-tuning effect.\" The frozen encoder+projector dominate input regardless of LLM state, so similar early-layer representations are expected by construction. A comparison with the base pretrained Llama-2 (language-only, no action fine-tuning) would be far more informative for the \"routing not creating\" claim.\n\n**Impact on claims:** Makes the \"training invariance\" finding (Claim 3) less surprising than presented. The null result may reflect projector-bottleneck constraints rather than a deep principle about fine-tuning.\n\n### W3. Claim 3 Rests on a Null Result Without Equivalence Testing (Both — Major)\nThe claim \"training does not change distributed representation structure\" relies on non-significance (p=0.20) with n=10 tasks and observed Δ=0.016. Statistical power to detect a true small effect is very low. Absence of evidence ≠ evidence of absence. The paper should report a Two One-Sided Tests (TOST) equivalence procedure with a pre-specified margin (e.g., Δ < 0.05) to affirmatively establish equivalence.\n\n### W4. Partially Confirmatory of Concurrent Work (R2 — Moderate)\nGrant et al. (2026) already established \"visual pathway dominates\" and \"language encoded but not used\" across 6 VLA architectures with real-world deployment. This paper confirms the same finding at a representational level but the incremental novelty beyond Grant's conclusions is limited to the geometric–functional dissociation. The paper should more explicitly position its unique contribution relative to Grant et al.\n\n### W5. OOD Confound (R1 — Moderate)\nThe trained model was fine-tuned on LIBERO-10 but analyzed on LIBERO-Goal (out-of-distribution). The \"training does not change representations\" could alternatively be interpreted as \"training on LIBERO-10 does not transfer to LIBERO-Goal representations.\" Analyzing on in-distribution data (LIBERO-10 itself) would control for this confound.\n\n### W6. Language Instruction Null Result Is Trivially Expected (R2 — Moderate)\nIn LIBERO, each scene uniquely determines the task, making instructions redundant. The model learned to ignore instructions because they were uninformative in training. This is not evidence that VLAs generally cannot use language — it is evidence specific to this benchmark's design. The paper should acknowledge this more explicitly.\n\n### W7. Missing Related Work Connections (R2 — Moderate)\nKey missing references:\n- **Aghajanyan et al. (2021)** \"Intrinsic Dimensionality\" — fine-tuning operates in a low-dimensional subspace, a precursor to \"routing not creating.\"\n- **Voita et al. (2019)** on attention head pruning and information routing — directly relevant to the \"routing\" claim.\n- **Huh et al. (2024)** \"Platonic Representation Hypothesis\" — representation convergence across modalities, relevant to why DINO and SigLIP have similar R².\n- **Hewitt & Manning (2019)** — probe complexity concerns the authors themselves identified but never formally addressed.\n- Broader probing literature in NLP (Belinkov & Glass 2019 survey).\n\n### W8. No Probe Complexity Ablation (R1 — Minor)\nThe MLP probe (Linear-ReLU-Linear, 256 hidden units) is a specific capacity choice. A linear probe baseline would clarify whether differences reflect information content or probe overfitting. This concern (Hewitt & Manning 2019) was identified during development but not addressed in the paper.\n\n### W9. Cross-Benchmark Replication Limitations (Both — Minor)\nLIBERO-Spatial and LIBERO-Goal share the same simulator, visual style, and related task structures. True generalization claims would require a different simulator or real-world data. The \"cross-benchmark ratios 0.95–0.97\" may reflect benchmark similarity rather than finding robustness.\n\n### W10. Copy Baseline and Autocorrelation (R1 — Minor)\nA copy baseline (a_{t+1} = a_t) achieves R²=0.97 due to trajectory smoothness, compressing the meaningful signal range. The absolute R² values (~0.70) may be substantially inflated by temporal autocorrelation. The meaningful differences (0.03–0.05 range) are small in absolute terms.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1 (R1, Critical):** Were the LIBERO-Spatial results (Tables 4–7) actually computed from executed experiments? If so, through which experiment run?\n\n**Q2 (Both, Major):** Have you considered running a TOST equivalence procedure for Claim 3, to affirmatively establish equivalence within a pre-specified margin rather than relying on non-significance?\n\n**Q3 (R2, Major):** What does the RSA look like for a base Llama-2 pretrained model (not fine-tuned for actions, not randomly initialized)? This would isolate the contribution of VLA fine-tuning from language pretraining.\n\n**Q4 (R1):** What is the R² of a linear probe (no hidden layer) at the projector and best LLM layer? This would address the Hewitt & Manning (2019) concern.\n\n**Q5 (R1):** Would the trained model show different results if analyzed on LIBERO-10 (in-distribution) rather than LIBERO-Goal (OOD)?\n\n**Q6 (R2):** The CKA ordering reversal (untrained > trained > projector) is described but not adequately explained. Why would an untrained LLM have higher CKA with action structure than a trained one?\n\n**Q7 (R1):** How sensitive are RSA results to the dissimilarity metric choice (cosine vs Euclidean vs correlation distance)?\n\n**Q8 (R2):** For the CLIP control: was the comparison controlled for representation dimensionality? CLIP and SigLIP have different embedding spaces.\n\n---\n\n## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\n| Planned Experiment | Covered in Paper? | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| CLIP-ViT Pretraining Control (plan_012) | Yes — Tables 1–2 | Fully covered, results match expected values |\n| LIBERO-Spatial Cross-Benchmark (plan_010) | Yes — Tables 4–7 | Results reported but execution status unclear from project infrastructure |\n\nBoth planned experiments appear in the paper. The CLIP control is convincing. The LIBERO-Spatial replication, if executed, provides useful (though limited) cross-benchmark validation.\n\n---\n\n## Suggestions for Improvement\n\n1. **Add one additional VLA architecture** (e.g., Octo) to justify claims about \"VLAs\" generally.\n2. **Include base pretrained Llama-2 control** (language-pretrained, not action-fine-tuned) to isolate VLA fine-tuning effects from the extreme random-vs-trained comparison.\n3. **Report TOST equivalence test** for Claim 3 with pre-specified margin.\n4. **Add linear probe baseline** alongside MLP probes.\n5. **Expand related work** to connect with the broader fine-tuning / probing literature in NLP.\n6. **Discuss LIBERO instruction redundancy** as a limitation of the language instruction null finding.\n7. **Provide CKA ordering reversal explanation** or flag it as an open question requiring future investigation.\n\n---\n\n## Final Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score |\n|---|---|\n| Novelty | 3.5/6 — One novel finding (geometric–functional dissociation); \"routing\" framing is a repackaging of known dynamics |\n| Soundness | 4.5/6 — Rigorous methods and statistics, but Claim 3 rests on underpowered null result |\n| Significance | 3.5/6 — Partially confirmatory of concurrent work; practical implications are incremental |\n| Clarity | 4.5/6 — Well-organized, clear tables, logical structure |\n| **Overall** | **4 (Weak Accept)** |\n| Confidence | 4/5 |\n\n**Justification:** The paper presents a systematic and methodologically careful representational analysis with one genuinely novel insight (geometric–functional dissociation between RSA and probe R² across encoder types). The experimental design with multiple controls is commendable. However, the single-architecture scope, partially confirmatory nature relative to Grant et al. (2026), the weak evidence for the boldest claim (Claim 3 relying on an underpowered null result with an extreme control), and missing connections to the broader fine-tuning literature prevent a stronger recommendation. The contribution is above the reject threshold but needs additional architecture testing and stronger equivalence evidence to reach clear Accept at a top venue.\n","project":"vla-ssd","tier":"best","dream_id":"20260516T011810","overall_score":"weak accept","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11714,"cache_read_input_tokens":52702,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11714},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f97ff991-0e8f-42e6-bed2-f413306c2d1e","uuid":"8c4ee4ce-9b45-44ef-9afe-d51cf2449140"}
